---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Charts & Color | tcplot: Data Visualization in Minneapolis and St. Paul"
    description="Bring a chart whose color you love or hate, then recolor charts together and present the before and after."
>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-8 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">
                Upcoming: Charts & Color
            </h2>

            <div class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
                <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">
                    Saturday, Jan 16 · 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM CST
                </p>
                <p class="flex items-start gap-2 mt-1">
                    <svg
                        class="w-5 h-5 mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-gray-400"
                        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                        fill="currentColor"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                    >
                        <path
                            fill-rule="evenodd"
                            d="M12 2c-3.866 0-7 3.07-7 6.857 0 4.34 4.686 9.86 6.412 11.74a.8.8 0 0 0 1.176 0C14.314 18.717 19 13.197 19 8.857 19 5.07 15.866 2 12 2Zm0 9.5a2.5 2.5 0 1 1 0-5 2.5 2.5 0 0 1 0 5Z"
                            clip-rule="evenodd"></path>
                    </svg>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Coffee Shop NE</span
                        ><br />2852 Johnson St NE · Minneapolis, MN</span
                    >
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Join tcplot for a morning about color in charts, in two acts,
                in the grayest month of the Minnesota year.
            </p>

            <div class="flex justify-center mt-12 mb-8">
                <div class="max-w-2xl w-full">
                    <a
                        href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blood_values_sorted_by_mass_and_molar_concentration.png"
                        target="_blank"
                        rel="noopener noreferrer"
                        class="block rounded-lg overflow-hidden aspect-[3/2] bg-white"
                    >
                        <img
                            src="/images/blood-test-reference-ranges.webp"
                            alt="A long chart of reference ranges for blood tests: dozens of overlapping bars in green, cyan, orange, red, pink, and violet on a log scale, labeled with hormones, vitamins, and proteins"
                            class="pan-chart w-full h-full object-cover"
                        />
                    </a>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mt-2">
                        Mikael Häggström, <em>Reference ranges for blood
                        tests</em> (2009): normal ranges for dozens of blood
                        tests on one log scale, by mass above and by molar
                        concentration below. Color does the sorting, and fading
                        bar ends show where a range gets fuzzy. Public domain
                        (CC0).
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Act one: bring a chart
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
                Your ticket in is one chart and two minutes on its color: a
                palette you love, a heatmap that finally made something click,
                a transit map you could navigate with your eyes half shut, or a
                rainbow colormap you love to hate. By the end of the round
                we'll have a wall of examples to steal from.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Act two: recolor
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                After a short primer on how color works in charts (sequential,
                diverging, and categorical palettes, why rainbows invent edges
                that aren't in the data, and how a chart looks to the one in
                twelve men with color vision deficiency), pick a chart, yours
                or one from our starter set, and recolor it. Then show the
                before and after. Or pull a palette from a January window in
                Minneapolis and make it work as a real chart palette.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                Pick your lane:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-lg text-gray-600 mb-8 list-disc pl-6">
                <li>
                    <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900">No-code:</span>
                    ColorBrewer, Viz Palette, Datawrapper
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Code:</span>
                    d3-scale-chromatic, chroma.js, matplotlib and viridis,
                    ggplot2
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                        >Accessibility:</span
                    > colorblindness simulators and contrast checkers
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Analog:</span>
                    paint chips, colored pencils, and paper
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Color in charts, briefly
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                The primer, in short. Most color decisions in a chart come down
                to matching the palette to the shape of the data.
            </p>

            <div class="space-y-8 mb-10">
                <div>
                    <h4 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                        Sequential: low to high
                    </h4>
                    <div class="h-8 rounded" style="background: linear-gradient(to right, #440154, #3b528b, #21918c, #5ec962, #fde725);" role="img" aria-label="A sequential palette running from dark purple through teal and green to bright yellow"></div>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3">
                        For ordered values with no special middle: temperature,
                        income, population density. Lightness should change
                        steadily from one end to the other, so darker reads as
                        more (or less) without a legend lookup. This one is
                        viridis, matplotlib's default since 2015.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div>
                    <h4 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                        Diverging: two directions from a middle
                    </h4>
                    <div class="h-8 rounded" style="background: linear-gradient(to right, #b2182b, #ef8a62, #fddbc7, #f7f7f7, #d1e5f0, #67a9cf, #2166ac);" role="img" aria-label="A diverging palette running from dark red through pale pink to near white, then pale blue to dark blue"></div>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3">
                        For data with a meaningful midpoint: above or below
                        zero, gains and losses, warmer or cooler than normal.
                        Two hues darken as they move away from a light, neutral
                        center. Put the center on the value that matters, not
                        just the middle of the range.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div>
                    <h4 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                        Categorical: different, not more
                    </h4>
                    <div class="h-8 rounded overflow-hidden flex" role="img" aria-label="Eight distinct colors: orange, sky blue, bluish green, yellow, blue, vermillion, reddish purple, and black"><div class="flex-1" style="background: #E69F00;"></div><div class="flex-1" style="background: #56B4E9;"></div><div class="flex-1" style="background: #009E73;"></div><div class="flex-1" style="background: #F0E442;"></div><div class="flex-1" style="background: #0072B2;"></div><div class="flex-1" style="background: #D55E00;"></div><div class="flex-1" style="background: #CC79A7;"></div><div class="flex-1" style="background: #000000;"></div></div>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3">
                        For groups with no order: species, neighborhoods,
                        parties. Hues should look equally important, so none
                        reads as a ranking. Past about seven categories, color
                        stops working; label directly or group the rest as
                        "other." This is the Okabe-Ito palette, designed to stay
                        distinct for colorblind readers.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div>
                    <h4 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                        Why rainbows invent edges
                    </h4>
                    <div class="h-8 rounded" style="background: linear-gradient(to right, #00007f, #0000ff, #007fff, #00ffff, #7fff7f, #ffff00, #ff7f00, #ff0000, #7f0000);" role="img" aria-label="A rainbow palette running from dark blue through cyan, green, and yellow to red and dark red"></div>
                    <div class="h-8 rounded mt-2" style="background: linear-gradient(to right, #00007f, #0000ff, #007fff, #00ffff, #7fff7f, #ffff00, #ff7f00, #ff0000, #7f0000); filter: grayscale(1);" role="img" aria-label="The same rainbow palette in grayscale, showing brightness rising to a peak in the middle and falling again"></div>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3">
                        The rainbow (above, then in grayscale) isn't ordered by
                        lightness: brightness climbs to a peak at cyan and
                        yellow, then falls again. Your eye reads those bright
                        bands as sharp boundaries, so smooth data looks like it
                        has edges, and the darkest blue and darkest red look
                        nearly the same. Compare viridis, which keeps getting
                        lighter the whole way.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div>
                    <h4 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                        One in twelve men
                    </h4>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                        About 1 in 12 men and 1 in 200 women of Northern
                        European ancestry have some color vision deficiency,
                        most often red-green. A chart that separates groups
                        only by red and green fails them. Vary lightness as
                        well as hue, label lines directly instead of relying on
                        a legend, and check your chart in a colorblindness
                        simulator before you share it.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                No design experience needed; the primer starts from zero. Bring
                one chart whose color you have opinions about (on a phone, a
                laptop, or paper) and, if you want to build digitally, a
                laptop.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                RSVP on <a
                    href="https://www.meetup.com/tcplot/"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Meetup</a
                >.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                Hope to see you there!
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>

<style>
    .pan-chart {
        animation: pan-chart 32s ease-in-out infinite alternate;
    }
    @keyframes pan-chart {
        from {
            object-position: 0% 50%;
        }
        to {
            object-position: 100% 50%;
        }
    }
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        .pan-chart {
            animation: none;
        }
    }
</style>
